# How to make custom structures

Custom structures allow you to add your own complex buildings into the world using custom world generation. Instead of spawning the structure as one thing, Minecraft allows you to break them into smaller pieces that allow for random generation (e.g. random village layouts, trial chambers). To save the "pieces", you use **Strucure Blocks**, and then you can load them using **Jigsaw Blocks**. 

## 1. Saving Pieces with Structure Blocks

To make a structure spawn in-game, you will need to first build and save one using a **Structure Block**.

1. Get a structure block: `/give @s structure_block`.
2. Place the structure block at the corner of your build and put it in **Save Mode**.
3. Give it a name using your namespace (e.g. `my_namespace:room_1`).
4. Go to the opposite corner of your build and place a second structure block. Put it in **Corner Mode** and give it the same name as you gave the first structure block.
5. Go back to the first structure block (the one in save mode) and click on "Detect". This will automatically detect the size (using the opposite corner block).
6. Verify the structure boundary is correct, then finally click the Save button.

This creates an `.nbt` file in your world save file. You will need to move this file into your datapack folder at `data/<namespace>/structure/<piece_name>.nbt`

## 2. Connecting Layouts with Jigsaw Blocks

Jigsaw blocks work like puzzle pieces. They connect different saved structure files together face-to-face.

Right-click a Jigsaw Block inside your build to set up its settings:

* **Name:** The ID of *this* connector point (e.g., `my_namespace:doorway`).
* **Target Name:** The jigsaw name it wants to connect to on another piece (e.g., `my_namespace:doorway`).
* **Pool:** The datapack path to the template pool file that holds the next pieces it can choose from (e.g., `my_namespace:room_pool`).
* **Turn into:** The block that replaces the Jigsaw block when the structure finishes spawning (usually `minecraft:air` so the path stays open).

You could also keep the **Turn into** empty to keep the Jigsaw Blocks to see if it connects correctly.

:::tip
Make sure the little arrow on the Jigsaw Block face points **outward** toward the empty space where the next piece should spawn!
:::

## 3. Randomizing Blocks with Processors

To stop structures from look exactly the same every time we, use **Structure Processors**. These are simple lists of rules that randomly swap out blocks when spawning (like changing 20% of your Stone Bricks into Mossy Stone Bricks).

Create your Processor file in your datapack at `data/<namespace>/worldgen/processor_list/<name>.json`

```json:data/my_namespace/worldgen/processor_list/ruin_decay.json
[
  {
    "processor_type": "minecraft:rule",
    "rules": [
      {
        "input_predicate": {
          "block": "minecraft:stone_bricks",
          "predicate_type": "minecraft:random_block_match",
          "probability": 0.2
        },
        "location_predicate": {
          "predicate_type": "minecraft:always_true"
        },
        "output_state": {
          "Name": "minecraft:mossy_stone_bricks"
        }
      },
      {
        "input_predicate": {
          "block": "minecraft:stone_bricks",
          "predicate_type": "minecraft:block_match"
        },
        "location_predicate": {
          "predicate_type": "minecraft:always_true"
        },
        "output_state": {
          "Name": "minecraft:stone_bricks"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
]

```

To use this logic, change `"processors": "minecraft:empty"` to your path (`"processors": "my_namespace:ruin_decay"`) inside your template pool configurations.

## 4. Creating Template Pools

Template pools are JSON lists that contain the pieces your structure can use. They tell the game which rooms are allowed to connect, and set the chance (weight - higher weight = more often) for each room to appear.

Create your pool file at
`data/<namespace>/worldgen/template_pool/<name>.json`

```json:data/my_namespace/worldgen/template_pool/room_pool.json
{
  "name": "my_namespace:room_pool",
  "fallback": "minecraft:empty",
  "elements": [
    {
      "weight": 3,
      "element": {
        "element_type": "minecraft:single_pool_element",
        "location": "my_namespace:room_standard",
        "projection": "rigid",
        "processors": "my_namespace:ruin_decay"
      }
    },
    {
      "weight": 1,
      "element": {
        "element_type": "minecraft:single_pool_element",
        "location": "my_namespace:room_treasure",
        "projection": "rigid",
        "processors": "my_namespace:ruin_decay"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## 5. Registering the Main Structure File

To tell the game to start spawning your layout, register the structure settings file at
`data/<namespace>/worldgen/structure/<name>.json`

```json:data/my_namespace/worldgen/structure/my_dungeon.json
{
  "type": "minecraft:jigsaw",
  "start_pool": "my_namespace:room_pool",
  "size": 7,
  "max_distance_from_center": 80,
  "biomes": "#minecraft:has_structure/mineshaft",
  "step": "surface_structures",
  "terrain_adaptation": "beard_thin",
  "start_height": {
    "absolute": 64
  },
  "project_start_to_heightmap": "WORLD_SURFACE_WG",
  "use_expansion_hack": false,
  "spawn_overrides": {}
}

```

## 6. In-Game Testing Commands

You can test your Jigsaw structures using in-game commands:

* Spawn the entire structure with `/place structure my_namespace:my_dungeon ~ ~ ~`
* To test just one pool connection, stand right next to an empty jigsaw connector block and type: `/place jigsaw my_namespace:room_pool my_namespace:doorway 1`.

You can also spawn it using a jigsaw block, but you'll have to put in the correct settings yourself.


:::warning
The /reload command does not reload worldgen files like structures or template pools. To see updates to your files, you must leave the world and rejoin it.
:::
